[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     51[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     52[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ### 1. `scanf("%d", ...)` reads across newlines
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     53[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     54[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `%d` skips *any* run of whitespace before the digits, so a newline is not a
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     55[0m:[38;5;10m[49m record separator the way it would be with `fgets`. `1\n2\n3`, `1\t2\r\n3` and
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     56[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `1\x0b2\x0c3` all yield three items. The Rust `Scanner::scan_int` skips the
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     57[0m:[38;5;10m[49m full C `isspace` set (`' '`, `\t`, `\n`, `\v`, `\f`, `\r`) before the sign, and
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     58[0m:[38;5;10m[49m byte `0x0e` is deliberately *not* in that set.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     59[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     60[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Probed by `scanf_reads_across_newlines_and_all_whitespace` and
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     61[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `whitespace_only_input_is_an_immediate_eof`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     62[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     63[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ### 2. Conversion overflow: saturate at `long`, then truncate to `int`
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     64[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     65[0m:[38;5;10m[49m glibc's `%d` converts with `strtol` and stores the result into an `int`. Two
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     66[0m:[38;5;10m[49m separate effects stack, and both are observable:
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     67[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     68[0m:[38;5;10m[49m * A value that fits in `long` but not in `int` is **truncated** (low 32 bits).
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     69[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   `2147483648` → `-2147483648`; `4294967296` → `0`; `4294967297` → `1`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     70[0m:[38;5;10m[49m * A value that overflows `long` **saturates** at `LONG_MAX` / `LONG_MIN` first
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     71[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   and is only then truncated. `99999999999999999999` → `LONG_MAX` → `-1`;
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     72[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   `-99999999999999999999` → `LONG_MIN` → `0`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     73[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     74[0m:[38;5;10m[49m A translation that used Rust's `str::parse::<i32>()` (which errors on overflow)
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     75[0m:[38;5;10m[49m would have broken out of the read loop here and printed nothing, and a
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     76[0m:[38;5;10m[49m translation that saturated directly at the `i32` limits would have printed
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     77[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `2147483647` / `-2147483648` instead. The Rust accumulates into `i64`, latches
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     78[0m:[38;5;10m[49m a `saturated` flag on overflow, then does `value as i32`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     79[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     80[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Probed by `conversion_overflow_truncates_and_saturates_like_the_c_does`,
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     81[0m:[38;5;10m[49m including 1000-digit inputs in both signs.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     82[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     83[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ### 3. A sign with no digit after it is a *matching failure*, not zero
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     84[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     85[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `-`, `+`, `-a`, `- 5`, `--1` and `+-1` all make `scanf` return 0, so the loop
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     86[0m:[38;5;10m[49m breaks and nothing is printed. Reproduced by requiring at least one digit after
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     87[0m:[38;5;10m[49m the optional sign, and by pushing the offending byte back (C's `ungetc`) rather
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     88[0m:[38;5;10m[49m than consuming it.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     89[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     90[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Probed by `sign_without_digits_is_a_matching_failure`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     91[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     92[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ### 4. Partial conversions stop at the first non-digit
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     93[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     94[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `5x` yields one item, `3.14` yields `3`, `0x10` yields `0` (stopping at `x`),
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     95[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `1e5` yields `1`, `1_2` yields `1`. `%d` is decimal-only — there is no hex or
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     96[0m:[38;5;10m[49m float handling.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     97[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     98[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Probed by `matching_failure_part_way_through`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     99[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    100[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ### 5. Aliased `fma_array` collapses to `v * v + v`
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    101[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    102[0m:[38;5;10m[49m The only call is `fma_array(out, out, out, out, len)`, so `mul1`, `mul2`, `add`
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    103[0m:[38;5;10m[49m and `out` are the same buffer. Within one iteration all three reads at index
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    104[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `i` happen before the write at index `i`, so each element becomes
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    105[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `v * v + v` — the aliasing does not change the per-element result, and the
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    106[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `const` qualifiers on the parameters do not imply no-alias. The Rust models
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    107[0m:[38;5;10m[49m this directly instead of taking four separate slices.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    108[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    109[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ### 6. Signed overflow wraps two's-complement
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    110[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    111[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `out[i] = v * v + v` overflows for most inputs (`46341 * 46341` already does),
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    112[0m:[38;5;10m[49m which is undefined behaviour in C. The compiled C wraps, so the Rust uses
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    113[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `wrapping_mul` / `wrapping_add`. Verified that the C binary's output is
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    114[0m:[38;5;10m[49m unchanged at `-O0`, `-O1`, `-O2`, `-O3` and `-Ofast`, so the observed wrapping
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    115[0m:[38;5;10m[49m is not an artefact of the unoptimised CMake default build.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    116[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    117[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Using plain `*` and `+` in Rust would additionally have *panicked* in a debug
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    118[0m:[38;5;10m[49m build (exit 101 with a stderr message) rather than printing a wrapped value.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    119[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Both the debug and release Rust binaries were tested and neither panics.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    120[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    121[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Probed by `arithmetic_around_the_overflow_boundary` and `int_boundaries`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    122[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    123[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ### 7. Only the first 100 items are read at all
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    124[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    125[0m:[38;5;10m[49m The loop guard `i < 100` stops the reads; the rest of stdin is never consumed.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    126[0m:[38;5;10m[49m So `1 .. 150` prints exactly 100 lines, and garbage placed after the hundredth
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    127[0m:[38;5;10m[49m item is invisible.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    128[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    129[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Probed by `input_longer_than_the_array_is_truncated_at_100`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    130[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    131[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ### 8. Output formatting
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    132[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    133[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `printf("%d\n", ...)` — one value per line, no field width, no padding, and a
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    134[0m:[38;5;10m[49m trailing newline after the last value. Output is empty (zero bytes, not a bare
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    135[0m:[38;5;10m[49m newline) when `len == 0`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    136[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    137[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ### 9. `int data[100]` is uninitialised in C
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    138[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    139[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Only `data[0..i]` is ever read, and each of those was written by a successful
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    140[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `scanf`, so the uninitialised bytes are never observable. Zero-initialising the
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    141[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Rust array is therefore safe.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    142[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    143[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ## Stream conditions also checked
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    144[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    145[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Compared outside the cargo suite, since they depend on how the shell wires up
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    146[0m:[38;5;10m[49m the file descriptors:
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    147[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    148[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | condition | C | Rust |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    149[0m:[38;5;10m[49m |---|---|---|
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    150[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | stdout redirected to `/dev/full` | exit 0, empty stderr | same |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    151[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | stdout file descriptor closed (`>&-`) | exit 0, empty stderr | same |
